<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antoine, Duke of Brabant</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1384">1384</a>–<a href="/years/1415">1415</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Anthony, Duke of Brabant · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-valois">House of Valois</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antoine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> was duke of Brabant and Limburg from 1406 until 1415, a younger son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a> of Burgundy. His great-aunt Duchess Joanna made him her heir, and he governed Brabant from 1404 and recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> for the duchy. He was killed fighting for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt">Battle of Agincourt</a> in 1415, and his sons John IV and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-saint-pol">Philip of Saint-Pol</a> succeeded him in turn.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antoine-duke-of-brabant.jpg" alt="Antoine, Duke of Brabant" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NumisAntica · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hertog_van_Brabant_Limburg_Anton_van_Bourgondie_-_AR_Groot_-_1-2_Botdrager_Leuven.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Anthony was born on 21 August 1384, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-iii-countess-of-flanders">Margaret III, Countess of Flanders</a>, and a brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-fearless">John the Fearless</a>. Before he came into Brabant he was Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rethel">Rethel</a>, from 1402 to 1406. On the death of his childless great-aunt Joanna in 1406 he inherited the duchies of Brabant, Lothier and Limburg, and from 1411 he was also co-duke of Luxemburg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a> on 21 February 1402 he married Jeanne of Saint-Pol, daughter of Waleran III of Luxembourg, Count of Ligny and Saint-Pol, who died in 1407. Their sons were John IV, Duke of Brabant, born in 1403, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-saint-pol">Philip of Saint-Pol</a>, born in 1404, who held the duchy from 1427 to 1430. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> on 16 July 1409 he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisabeth-of-gorlitz">Elisabeth of Görlitz</a>, Duchess of Luxembourg, daughter of John, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorlitz">Görlitz</a>; they had no children. He also had a daughter, Anna of Brabant, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-peralta">Pedro de Peralta</a> y Ezpeleta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Anthony reached the field of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt">Agincourt</a> late on 25 October 1415. In his haste he put on the improvised armour of his chamberlain and a surcoat made from a trumpeter's flag, and charged with a few retainers, crying Brabant! Brabant! At the English lines he was knocked down and captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>. He was then killed by a cut to the throat with the other prisoners whose execution King Henry V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> ordered; the English did not know his rank or ransom value because of his makeshift surcoat.</p><p>The order came while the smaller English army was stretched to its limits guarding prisoners with the battle not yet won. An attack on the king's baggage train led by Ysembart d'Azincourt is thought to have driven Henry to the decision, and some chroniclers gave Anthony's late charge as its cause. The killing stopped once the attack was seen to falter.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anthony of Brabant” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duchess Joanna, childless, passed the duchy to her great-nephew <b>Antoine of Burgundy</b>, a younger son of Philip the Bold, who was killed at the Battle of Agincourt in 1415; after his sons John IV and Philip of Saint-Pol died without heirs, Philip the Good of Burgundy inherited Brabant…</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Azincourt, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.46,2.13&amp;year=1404" aria-label="Azincourt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4599 411 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Antoine, Duke of Brabant"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5049 681h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5049 681h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.46,2.13&amp;year=1404">What was here in 1404? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
